# topology
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  What happens inside classify_comms() step by step
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Input: node_gpus=[2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2, dp_outside=True (default)

  The function answers one question for each comm type:
  "If we run training with this (pp, tp, dp) on this cluster, does each group
   communicate over PCIe (fast) or over the Ethernet switch (slow)?"

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  CRITICAL: dp_outside changes the rank layout completely
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  HybridParallelPlugin builds a ProcessGroupMesh using np.ravel_multi_index
  (C-order = LAST axis varies fastest).  The mesh shape depends on dp_outside:

    dp_outside=True  (DEFAULT, what HybridParallelPlugin uses by default):
      mesh shape = (dp, pp, tp)
      rank = dp_rank × (pp × tp) + pp_rank × tp + tp_rank

    dp_outside=False:
      mesh shape = (pp, dp, tp)
      rank = pp_rank × (dp × tp) + dp_rank × tp + tp_rank

  These two layouts assign ranks to VERY DIFFERENT physical GPUs.
  The topology module defaults to dp_outside=True to match HybridParallelPlugin.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 1 — Build the rank→node map
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  node_gpus = [2, 4, 2]  means:
    node 0 (node18, 10.10.10.18)  2 GPUs  → ranks 0, 1
    node 1 (node20, 10.10.10.20)  4 GPUs  → ranks 2, 3, 4, 5
    node 2 (node16, 10.10.10.16)  2 GPUs  → ranks 6, 7

  rank_to_node = [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2]
                  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑
                  r0 r1 r2 r3 r4 r5 r6 r7

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 2 — Compute global rank from (dp_rank, pp_rank, tp_rank)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dp_outside=True  →  rank = dp_rank × (pp × tp) + pp_rank × tp + tp_rank
  For pp=2, tp=2, dp=2  →  pp×tp = 4:

  dp_rank  pp_rank  tp_rank  →  global_rank  →  node
     0        0        0           0               0  (node18)
     0        0        1           1               0  (node18)
     0        1        0           2               1  (node20)
     0        1        1           3               1  (node20)
     1        0        0           4               1  (node20)
     1        0        1           5               1  (node20)
     1        1        0           6               2  (node16)
     1        1        1           7               2  (node16)

  Key insight of dp_outside=True:
    DP replica 0 (dp_rank=0) gets ranks 0-3: contains its own complete pp pipeline
    DP replica 1 (dp_rank=1) gets ranks 4-7: contains its own complete pp pipeline
    Each replica's pipeline stages are consecutive → tend to land on the same node.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 3 — Classify TP groups
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A TP group = same (dp_rank, pp_rank), tp_rank varies over [0..tp).

  dp=0 pp=0:  ranks [0, 1]  →  nodes [0, 0]  →  intra-node ✓
  dp=0 pp=1:  ranks [2, 3]  →  nodes [1, 1]  →  intra-node ✓
  dp=1 pp=0:  ranks [4, 5]  →  nodes [1, 1]  →  intra-node ✓
  dp=1 pp=1:  ranks [6, 7]  →  nodes [2, 2]  →  intra-node ✓

  All 4 groups are intra-node  →  tp_intra_node = True

  Pruning rule: tp > min(node_gpus) = 2 → guaranteed cross-node TP → pruned.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 4 — Classify PP boundaries
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A PP boundary = adjacent (pp_rank, pp_rank+1) with the same (dp_rank, tp_rank).

  dp=0 tp=0:  rank 0(n0) → rank 2(n1)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  dp=0 tp=1:  rank 1(n0) → rank 3(n1)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  dp=1 tp=0:  rank 4(n1) → rank 6(n2)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  dp=1 tp=1:  rank 5(n1) → rank 7(n2)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗

  At least one is cross-node  →  pp_intra_node = False

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 5 — Classify DP groups
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A DP group = same (pp_rank, tp_rank), dp_rank varies over [0..dp).

  pp=0 tp=0:  ranks [0, 4]  →  nodes [0, 1]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  pp=0 tp=1:  ranks [1, 5]  →  nodes [0, 1]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  pp=1 tp=0:  ranks [2, 6]  →  nodes [1, 2]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  pp=1 tp=1:  ranks [3, 7]  →  nodes [1, 2]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗

  At least one is cross-node  →  dp_intra_node = False

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Final result for (pp=2, tp=2, dp=2) on [2, 4, 2]
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ┌───────────┬────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │ Comm type │         Group example          │           Result             │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ TP        │ {0,1}, {2,3}, {4,5}, {6,7}     │ ✓ intra-node (PCIe/NVLink)   │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ PP        │ 0→2, 1→3, 4→6, 5→7            │ ✗ cross-node (100 GbE)       │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ DP        │ {0,4}, {1,5}, {2,6}, {3,7}     │ ✗ cross-node (100 GbE)       │
  └───────────┴────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘

  TopologyInfo(tp_intra_node=True, pp_intra_node=False, dp_intra_node=False)

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Why dp_outside matters: (pp=2, tp=1, dp=4) comparison
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  With dp_outside=True (CORRECT — HybridParallelPlugin default):
    rank = dp_rank × 2 + pp_rank
    dp=0: ranks 0,1 → node0,node0  ← stage 0 and stage 1 are BOTH on node18
    dp=1: ranks 2,3 → node1,node1  ← stage 0 and stage 1 are BOTH on node20
    dp=2: ranks 4,5 → node1,node1
    dp=3: ranks 6,7 → node2,node2

    PP boundaries: all INTRA-node  →  pp_intra_node = True  ← cheap PP!
    DP groups: all CROSS-node      →  dp_intra_node = False ← expensive DP

  With dp_outside=False (WRONG for default HybridParallelPlugin):
    rank = pp_rank × 4 + dp_rank
    pp=0: ranks 0,1,2,3 → stage 0 on all 4 nodes/groups
    pp=1: ranks 4,5,6,7 → stage 1 on all 4 nodes/groups
    dp=0: ranks 0,4 → node0,node1 ← cross-node PP

    PP boundaries: all CROSS-node  →  pp_intra_node = False ← expensive PP
    DP groups: nodes 0,0 / 1,1 / ...  →  some INTRA-node, some CROSS

  The physical layout is COMPLETELY DIFFERENT.
  Using the wrong formula would misclassify PP comm cost for this plan
  (21 ms expensive vs 0.28 ms cheap).

  This is why topology.classify_comms() has a dp_outside parameter that must
  match the dp_outside parameter of HybridParallelPlugin. Default True for both.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  How dp_outside=True affects the full search table
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Correct table (dp_outside=True) for [2,4,2] cluster:

  plan              total ms   compute  bubble  TP comm  PP comm  DP comm  pp_i  dp_i
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  pp=1 tp=1 dp=8    561.2 ms    32.0     0.0      0.0      0.0    529.2    T     F
  pp=1 tp=2 dp=4    247.3 ms    16.0     0.0      4.5      0.0    226.8    T     F
  pp=2 tp=1 dp=4    247.1 ms    16.0     4.0      0.0      0.3    226.8    T     F  ← PP now cheap!
  pp=2 tp=2 dp=2    109.2 ms     8.0     2.0      2.3     21.3     75.6    F     F
  pp=4 tp=1 dp=2    110.9 ms     8.0     6.0      0.0     21.3     75.6    F     F  ← DP now expensive!
  pp=4 tp=2 dp=1 *   29.4 ms     4.0     3.0      1.1     21.3      0.0    F     T
  pp=8 tp=1 dp=1     32.3 ms     4.0     7.0      0.0     21.3      0.0    F     T

  Old WRONG table (dp_outside=False) would have shown:
    pp=2 tp=1 dp=4:  PP comm = 21.3 ms  (overestimated — PP was actually intra-node)
    pp=4 tp=1 dp=2:  DP comm =  1.0 ms  (underestimated — DP was actually cross-node)

  The winner (pp=4 tp=2 dp=1) is the same, but the scores for other plans
  were wrong with the old formula. Correct scores matter for making the right
  trade-off when model size or microbatch count changes.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Unit test (no cluster needed)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  from colossalai.auto_parallel.hybrid_planner.topology import classify_comms, describe

  # Test 1: main cluster plan (pp=2,tp=2,dp=2) — same regardless of dp_outside
  t_out = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2, dp_outside=True)
  t_in  = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2, dp_outside=False)
  assert t_out == t_in  # happens to be same for this specific plan
  assert t_out.tp_intra_node == True
  assert t_out.pp_intra_node == False

  # Test 2: (pp=2,tp=1,dp=4) — dp_outside MATTERS here
  t_out = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=1, dp=4, dp_outside=True)
  assert t_out.pp_intra_node == True   # each DP replica's pipeline is co-located
  assert t_out.dp_intra_node == False  # DP peers span nodes
  t_in  = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=1, dp=4, dp_outside=False)
  assert t_in.pp_intra_node == False   # DIFFERENT: PP spans nodes with wrong formula

  # Print full table
  print(describe([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=1, dp=4, dp_outside=True))

